From: Rosen Penev <rosenp@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: b43: send and handle the TX status without wl->mutex

b43 sends frames from a work item, as the DMA rings are protected by
wl->mutex, and handles the TX status in the interrupt thread. Every
frame mac80211 hands over wakes the TX work, and every TX status the
interrupt thread, almost three context switches per forwarded frame.

With DMA and without SDIO, protect the TX path with a spinlock instead:
send the frames right where mac80211 hands them over, and handle the TX
status of interrupts that signal nothing else in the NAPI poll. As
mac80211 can send frames from within the TX status, a frame handed over
while the lock is held is sent by the holder once it releases the lock.

Two things on the TX path sleep, and move to a work item that holds
wl->mutex: the N-PHY TX power control register access before OFDM
frames, which suspends the MAC, and the periodic TX power check after a
TX status. When the base indices of the TX power control are to be
loaded after idle, OFDM frames wait for the work, as they would
compress the PA otherwise.

On an Asus RT-N16 (BCM4716) sending to a BCM4331 client, 4 runs each,
medians for TCP:

                       before        after
  UDP forwarded at 15 Mbit/s:
    cycles/frame       259-265K      203-205K
    instructions/frame 29.9-30.4K    25.9-26.1K
    context switches   2.80-2.94     0.08-0.09
  TCP forwarded        51.1          65.0 Mbit/s
  TCP from the router  59.8          67.8 Mbit/s

Receiving is unchanged. The airtime fairness between a client at HT
MCS 15 and one at 6 Mbit/s stays the same (22.6-24.8 and 2.8-3.0 Mbit/s
at 30 Mbit/s of UDP to each), and frames go out normally after idle.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Rosen Penev <rosenp@gmail.com>
